{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1553372507,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTkwMTU2OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3265#issuecomment-475901569"},"message":"\u003e - ideally, each interim commit should record in its message that it is a part of the \"bigger run\".\n\nHow would that work?  We don't have control over what the command is committing.\n\n\u003e - at the end of the run, if current commit is not the original commit, placing a field in the `run` record, like `parent` containing this commit\n\u003e [...]\n\u003e Then we get only the issue for humans to easily identify that a commit was a part of the larger `run`, which I would find highly desired. (I do look at them and find useful)\n\n\nI haven't given it too much thought, but an alternative would be to use the branch structure to indicate multi-commit runs.  Glossing over some HEAD management, this could be something like\n\n  * Record initial commit.\n  * Run the command and notice that we're no longer on the initial commit.\n  * Save run results as usual.\n  * Merge temporary run branch.\n\n```\n$ datalad run -m'a committing command' \\\n  'git commit --allow-empty -mmsg \u0026\u0026 touch foo'\n\nM   [DATALAD RUNCMD] a committing command\n|\\\n| R [DATALAD RUNCMD] a committing command\n| o msg\n|/\no \u003cstarting commit\u003e\n```\n\nCommit R would be created because this particular command leaves uncommitted changes.  M and R could both have json records, but M should always because R may not exist.\n\nSuch a structure would make it easy for both human and machine inspectors to identify multi-commit runs.\n\n[ Relates to #3096. ]","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553436815,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTk2MzM2Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3265#issuecomment-475963366"},"message":"I like branch/merge idea -- probably it is the way to go!  Not sure though if leftover changes should be committed with the same record/message. Could cause confusion.  I would have committed R as \"a committing command [unsaved changes]\" and then have M with the run record\n\n\u003e \u003e * ideally, each interim commit should record in its message that it is a part of the \"bigger run\".\n\u003e \n\u003e How would that work? We don't have control over what the command is committing.\n\n- `run` defines DATALAD_RUN_RECORD env variable\n- `GitRepo.commit` (or at higher `save` level?) appends it to the commit message if variable is defined.\n\nMight relate also to #3236 (automated commit messages). May be such a variable would be `DATALAD_COMMIT_INTENT_MESSAGE` or alike, so top level command defines it to be used in underlying commits (including for git-annex itself).","files":null}]}